WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.960
You have to find something to like
in the language you're learning.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.580
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here once
again talking about language learning,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.720
and I want to talk about what I
would call indirect motivation.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.110
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.060
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.440
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:23.460
We do appreciate it.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.850
So we know that we need to be motivated.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.020
I say this all the time.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.790
I hope my videos are motivating.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.320
And everyone understands that if you have
a motivated student, when it comes to

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.960
language learning and probably anything
else, that person will learn on their own.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.650
So motivation is the key,
but how do we get motivated?

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.490
So, you know, I was thinking
that, uh, you know, I mentioned...

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.620
I did a video about my
interest in history.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:54.750
So I ended up motivated, motivating
myself to learn a language because

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.730
I'm motivated to learn history.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:02.985
So in a sense, the learning of the
language is almost an indirect motivation.

00:01:02.985 --> 00:01:05.804
It's not because I want to learn
the language it's I'm interested

00:01:05.804 --> 00:01:09.884
in history and therefore I end up
learning the language and, you know,

00:01:09.884 --> 00:01:12.255
that's true with so much in life.

00:01:12.854 --> 00:01:21.255
Um, if you say, I want to make a lot of
money, uh, maybe you first have to provide

00:01:21.255 --> 00:01:26.535
a service, uh, which other people are
willing to pay for, uh, or maybe you have

00:01:26.535 --> 00:01:32.985
to be so skilled at what you do, that
you can generate a revenue stream or you

00:01:32.985 --> 00:01:36.405
have to be so knowledgeable, in other
words, you have to be good at what you

00:01:36.405 --> 00:01:41.625
do so that the direct motivation, even
though what you want to do out there is

00:01:41.745 --> 00:01:47.745
make money you have to first build up
your knowledge base, your skill base.

00:01:48.195 --> 00:01:54.525
Uh, and you have to have an attitude that
says I'm gonna deliver, you know, value.

00:01:55.305 --> 00:01:55.605
Okay.

00:01:55.665 --> 00:02:00.065
I'm gonna, if you want to be a
good, uh, Shoemaker to take a

00:02:00.075 --> 00:02:03.615
simple example, you, you, you
can't just be motivated for money.

00:02:03.705 --> 00:02:07.245
You actually have to enjoy making shoes.

00:02:07.635 --> 00:02:08.175
You have to...

00:02:08.205 --> 00:02:13.785
the same if you're in the job and your
goal is to do well in the job and to be

00:02:13.785 --> 00:02:18.915
promoted, uh, or you're providing some
service and you want to do well with

00:02:18.915 --> 00:02:22.305
your, you have a company of your own or
you're working for someone else, you have

00:02:22.305 --> 00:02:27.540
to do that job well, that's the direct
objective that you have to achieve.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:33.959
You have to do that well in order to
achieve the sort of indirect, uh, you

00:02:33.959 --> 00:02:39.899
know, goal, that will be the consequence
of your commitment to doing the job

00:02:39.899 --> 00:02:44.459
well, or to making sure you have the
skills and the knowledge that you need in

00:02:44.459 --> 00:02:47.190
order to achieve that longer term goal.

00:02:47.459 --> 00:02:51.090
Even in personal relationships uh,
you can't just go up to someone

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.510
and say, I want to be your friend,
or I want to be your lover.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:00.630
Uh, you first have to invest some
time and effort into getting to

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:04.710
know that person, uh, giving that
person a chance to get to know you.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:08.730
Uh, they have to like you, you have
to like them, you know, at that

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:15.150
level, you build up a connection and
emotional connection or a connection

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:22.230
of respect, uh, which then will lead
to the result, the long-term but

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.710
in a way indirect result, that that
person and you will become friends or

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.329
lovers or whatever the case may be.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.480
So there's no sort of short circuit
there's no, you know, jumping ahead

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:39.540
of yourself, you have to first find
that short term direct goal that

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.079
you can focus on that's going to
lead you to your longer term goal.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.180
Maybe I'm not making any sense here.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.430
When I bring it back to language
learning, what does it mean?

00:03:48.375 --> 00:03:52.335
You have to find something to like
in the language you're learning.

00:03:53.025 --> 00:03:57.495
Uh, if you cannot find anything to
like, anything of interest, you're

00:03:57.495 --> 00:03:58.935
going to have a very tough time.

00:03:58.935 --> 00:04:01.695
You can't just say, I would
like to be fluent in English

00:04:01.725 --> 00:04:03.525
because I need it for my job.

00:04:03.825 --> 00:04:06.855
I would like to be fluent in
German or Chinese, because it's

00:04:06.855 --> 00:04:08.055
going to be good for my career.

00:04:08.535 --> 00:04:13.305
You actually have to find something
in Chinese culture, in German culture

00:04:13.305 --> 00:04:17.745
and eh, whatever Japanese, French,
Russia, something that you like.

00:04:18.315 --> 00:04:19.695
You don't have to like everything.

00:04:20.265 --> 00:04:24.195
Uh, similarly it helps if you
find a friend, but you don't have

00:04:24.195 --> 00:04:27.495
to like all Chinese, Russians,
Japanese, French, you name it.

00:04:27.674 --> 00:04:31.424
But you have to find some people
that you like, you have to find some

00:04:31.424 --> 00:04:33.615
aspects of the language that you like.

00:04:33.914 --> 00:04:36.405
I often quote this expression in French...

00:04:38.445 --> 00:04:43.575
in other words, we, our appetite develops
as we are eating, but that's assume...

00:04:43.575 --> 00:04:45.875
that assumes that you're
eating something that you like.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.880
So, uh, you know, you have to
find that something, and that

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.140
takes a bit of looking around.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:56.220
It takes a bit of patience and
unfortunately it's often difficult.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:02.010
I have found it difficult at times to
find content that I'm interested in

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:07.570
at my level in the language, because I
know that if I can engage myself with

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:12.030
content of interest, the learning of
the language will look after itself.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:15.690
If I do enough listening and reading
on things of interest, but I have

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:19.560
to find those things of interest and
in some languages that's difficult

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:24.730
to do because you either have a
lot of very uninteresting beginner

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:28.740
material, or you have material
that is, you know, intended for the

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.210
native speaker and is too difficult.

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:34.260
And it's sort of hard to find
content that's going to lead you

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:38.790
towards the ultimate, you know,
most satisfying type of material.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.980
I think that's something that
we want to do better at LingQ.

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:46.440
Uh, we're trying to bring in, uh, a
greater variety of material from different

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.310
sources, including YouTube, where
people just have to click to get it.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:54.330
They don't have to search for it so
that they can kind of just fall into

00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:01.215
things of interest to them and make that
commitment to learning about those things

00:06:01.215 --> 00:06:06.224
of interest, that commitment to enjoying
whatever it is that they're interested in.

00:06:07.455 --> 00:06:12.974
And then recognizing that by doing that,
the longer-term goal of becoming fluent

00:06:12.974 --> 00:06:14.685
in the language will take care of itself.

00:06:14.865 --> 00:06:18.974
So, you know, it's perhaps a bit
of a confusing rant here, but it's

00:06:18.974 --> 00:06:22.425
something that I have felt all along
in my life, whether in my professional

00:06:22.425 --> 00:06:26.235
life, when I've often felt, if you do
your job well, you can't just say, I

00:06:26.235 --> 00:06:27.974
want to succeed, I want to make money.

00:06:28.125 --> 00:06:34.410
No, you actually have to invest in
providing uh, service, uh, even working

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:37.530
for a large corporation, you have to
give before you can get, you have to

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:41.580
give more, you can't sort of "when his
coffee break?", uh, "what's in it for

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:47.070
me?", you actually have to make that
investment in providing, you know, service

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:51.300
or working hard or developing your skills
in order to achieve that longer-term

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:53.430
goal of success or money or whatever.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:54.950
And the same with language learning.

00:06:55.245 --> 00:06:59.085
You have to commit yourself to something
of interest and your devouring those

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:03.075
things of interest and your language
skills will develop as a result.

00:07:03.105 --> 00:07:04.605
So it's something that I've experienced.

00:07:05.145 --> 00:07:06.675
I don't know if this makes sense.

00:07:06.705 --> 00:07:08.955
I hope it is encouraging.

00:07:09.345 --> 00:07:15.255
And for people who maybe are at a sort
of a doldrums stage where they feel

00:07:15.255 --> 00:07:20.475
they're not progressing and, uh, you
know, they're not, uh, enjoying the

00:07:20.475 --> 00:07:25.200
process, try a little harder to find
something that's going to turn your crank.

00:07:25.229 --> 00:07:27.090
That's going to be, you know, interesting.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:33.840
So that it'll take you to the final goal,
which is the fluency and the comfort

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.190
level in the language you're learning.

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:36.810
So there you have it.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.740
Thank you for listening and I'll
leave a couple of videos that

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.159
might be of interest to you.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:42.750
Thank you, bye for now.

